If Jane is guilty, the evidence pattern you list would be fairly plausible: repeated similarities in circumstances plus medical findings suggestive of smothering/abuse are the kinds of things one might expect to observe.

That said, not every guilty case would show all of these features:
- two incidents might differ in age, timing, or surrounding circumstances;
- some smothering cases leave little or no clear pathology;
- retrospective reinterpretation of Andy’s findings may be less certain than contemporaneous findings in Ben;
- the claim that Ben’s hypoxic injury occurred hours before death depends on expert accuracy.

So under a guilty hypothesis, this package of evidence seems substantially more likely than under a not-guilty hypothesis, but still not inevitable.

My rough judgment is that it is quite likely, though not close to certain.

74
